From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Nov 2026 16:45:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] include/linux: define 128-bit types for newer arm64 UAPI

Linux 7.3 changed the arm64 UAPI to declare the fpsimd registers in
struct fpsimd_context as __u128 (from <linux/types.h>) instead of
__uint128_t. glibc's <signal.h> includes <asm/sigcontext.h>, which
includes <linux/types.h> for that type.

U-Boot's host tools are built with -include include/libfdt_env.h. That
header includes this bundled <linux/types.h>, which defines
_LINUX_TYPES_H and thereby hides the system header that carries
__u128. proftool.c includes <sys/param.h>, and glibc's version pulls
in <signal.h>, so the host tool fails to build on arm64:

  asm/sigcontext.h:81:9: error: unknown type name '__u128'

Define __s128/__u128 the same way the kernel UAPI does when the
compiler supports 128-bit integers.

Signed-off-by: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
---
 include/linux/types.h | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/linux/types.h b/include/linux/types.h
--- a/include/linux/types.h
+++ b/include/linux/types.h
@@ -9,6 +9,17 @@
 #include <asm/types.h>
 #include <stdbool.h>
 
+/*
+ * Linux 7.3+ arm64 UAPI headers declare the fpsimd registers with
+ * __u128. glibc's <signal.h> includes <asm/sigcontext.h>, which
+ * expects that type from <linux/types.h>; this bundled header hides
+ * the system one, so provide the same definitions here.
+ */
+#ifdef __SIZEOF_INT128__
+__extension__ typedef __signed__ __int128 __s128 __attribute__((aligned(16)));
+__extension__ typedef unsigned __int128 __u128 __attribute__((aligned(16)));
+#endif
+
 #ifndef __KERNEL_STRICT_NAMES
 
 typedef __kernel_fd_set		fd_set;
